From: Zhenyao Mo <zmo@chromium.org>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 10:29:13 -0700
Subject: Ensure CompoundImageBacking::GetGpuBacking() validates latest content
 ID

When multiple GPU backings exist (e.g. with dynamic backing allocation
where the initial lazy GPU backing is resolved and rejected by
SupportsAccess() before a compatible backing is dynamically allocated),
GetGpuBacking() previously returned the first non-memory element in
|elements_| regardless of its content_id_.

Because CreateBackingFromBackingFactory() marks newly created GPU
backings as cleared when a shared memory backing is present, calling
CopyToGpuMemoryBuffer() or CopyToGpuMemoryBufferAsync() could select the
uninitialized or stale GPU backing instead of the active backing holding
latest_content_id_, copying uninitialized VRAM into shared memory.

Update GetGpuBacking() to check HasLatestContent(element) so only a GPU
backing holding the latest content is selected as the readback source,
and guard against null gpu_backing dereferences in error logging when no
GPU backing holds the latest content.

To cover this, CreateSharedMemoryForTesting() takes an optional
SharedImageFactoryRef. Supplying one makes the dynamic allocation branch
of GetOrAllocateBacking() reachable from the unit test, so the
regression test reproduces the bug entirely through the public API: a
single ProduceSkia() call resolves the initial GPU element, rejects it
via SupportsAccess(), and dynamically allocates a second element, and a
Skia BeginScopedWriteAccess() then makes that second element the holder
of the latest content. No test code mutates CompoundImageBacking state
directly.

SharedImageFactory itself needs no GL context here: its constructor
returns early once GL is reported as disabled, and registering the
existing TestSharedImageBackingFactory via
RegisterSharedImageBackingFactoryForTesting() makes dynamic allocations
produce TestImageBackings. That process-global GL override lives in a
dedicated fixture so it applies only to these two tests.

